{"name":"Shui Jiao Dumpling","hex":"#dccca3","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jiao","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A currency unit of the People's Republic of China, one tenth of a yuan."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jiao"},{"term":"dumpling","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A ball of dough that is cooked and may have a filling and/or additional ingredients in the dough. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}\n Specifically, a ball of dough used in stews and other sauced dishes; a thick noodle.\n Specifically, a food composed of a dough wrapper around a filling.","Specifically, a ball of dough used in stews and other sauced dishes; a thick noodle."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dumpling"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jiaozi","blocks":[{"text":"Jiaozi are a type of Chinese dumpling. They typically consist of a ground meat or vegetable filling wrapped into a thinly rolled piece of dough, which is then sealed by pressing the edges together. They can be boiled, steamed, pan-fried, deep-fried, or baked, and are traditionally served with a black vinegar and soy sauce dip. They can also be served in a soup. Jiaozi have great cultural significance within China. They are one of the major dishes eaten during the Chinese New Year throughout northern China and eaten all year round in the northern provinces. Their resemblance to the gold and silver ingots (sycee) used in Imperial China has meant that they symbolize wealth and good fortune."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/%E5%8F%B0%E7%81%A3%E5%8D%97%E6%8A%95%E8%8D%89%E5%B1%AF%E6%B0%B4%E9%A4%83Nantou%2C_Taiwan_Caotun_dumplings.jpg/330px-%E5%8F%B0%E7%81%A3%E5%8D%97%E6%8A%95%E8%8D%89%E5%B1%AF%E6%B0%B4%E9%A4%83Nantou%2C_Taiwan_Caotun_dumplings.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jiaozi"},{"title":"Dumpling","blocks":[{"text":"Dumplings are a broad class of dishes that consist of pieces of cooked dough, often wrapped around a filling. The dough can be based on bread, wheat or other flours, or potatoes, and it may be filled with meat, fish, tofu, cheese, vegetables, or a combination. Dumplings may be prepared using a variety of cooking methods and are found in many world cuisines."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/HK_SYP_%E8%A5%BF%E7%87%9F%E7%9B%A4_Sai_Ying_Pun_Kwan_Yick_Building_%E5%90%8D%E6%98%9F%E6%B5%B7%E9%AE%AE%E9%85%92%E5%AE%B6_Star_Seafood_Restaurant_food_%E5%90%9E%E9%BA%B5_Wonton_dim_sum_October_2020_SS2_01.jpg/330px-HK_SYP_%E8%A5%BF%E7%87%9F%E7%9B%A4_Sai_Ying_Pun_Kwan_Yick_Building_%E5%90%8D%E6%98%9F%E6%B5%B7%E9%AE%AE%E9%85%92%E5%AE%B6_Star_Seafood_Restaurant_food_%E5%90%9E%E9%BA%B5_Wonton_dim_sum_October_2020_SS2_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dumpling"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Tang_dynasty_pottery_dumplings.jpg/500px-Tang_dynasty_pottery_dumplings.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Tang_dynasty_pottery_dumplings.jpg/1280px-Tang_dynasty_pottery_dumplings.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Tang_dynasty_pottery_dumplings.jpg/500px-Tang_dynasty_pottery_dumplings.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pottery dumpling and delicacies from a Tang-dynasty tomb","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATang_dynasty_pottery_dumplings.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Cooking_with_a_wok_on_an_outdoor_stove_3.jpg/500px-Cooking_with_a_wok_on_an_outdoor_stove_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, http